<p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-primary/80 font-medium italic mb-8 border-l-4 border-secondary pl-6">A "herniated" or displaced TMJ disc is the most common cause of jaw clicking, locking, and pain. In plain terms, the small shock-absorbing disc inside the jaw joint has slipped forward out of its normal position, producing clicks, catching, or a stuck jaw. With the right workup and the right treatment, most patients get lasting relief — often without open surgery.</p>
<p>A herniated TMJ disc is not a structural rupture the way a slipped spinal disc is. It is a positional shift of the joint's fibrocartilage disc, and the correct medical term is <strong>internal derangement</strong>. Disc displacement is one of the most common findings we see at <a href="../index.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">Gnathos Facial Surgery</a>, particularly in patients between 20 and 45. If your jaw clicks, locks, or hurts in front of the ear, the disc is usually the prime suspect.</p>
